{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ROOT CAUSE (investigated before implementing; the ticket's stated mechanism is NOT what happens).\n\n1. The close path already runs the LLM verifier OUTSIDE the write lock, and has since the\n   gate was introduced (85a77b9cc8, 2026-06-18). `transition_close.close_ticket` calls\n   `close_precheck._completion_precheck` (which runs `llm.verify_completion`) BEFORE\n   `txn.transition_core`, and `transition_core` is where `lock.acquire` is first taken.\n   An optimistic re-check under the lock also already exists (`pre_status_check` /\n   `plan_review_recheck`), and `transition_core` re-reduces under the lock and raises\n   ConcurrencyMismatch (exit 10) on a status mismatch. So the requested restructure\n   (verify -> acquire -> re-check -> append -> release) is the code as it stands.\n\n2. Instrumented proof. Wrapping `_store.lock.acquire` / `LockHandle.release` around a full\n   `transition_compute(... in_progress -> closed)` with a stubbed verifier shows exactly four\n   short acquisitions, ALL after the verifier returns, each bracketing only a store mutation:\n   completion sidecar append, `txn.transition_core`, the SIGNATURE append, and\n   `_compact_on_close`. A `write_lock_is_busy()` probe called from inside the stubbed verifier\n   returns False (no lock dir, no ownership stamp).\n\n3. What the real long holder is: COMPACT-ON-CLOSE. Caught live on this host, tracker\n   .tickets-tracker (745M, ~4000 ticket dirs), pid 85578\n   `rebar transition ethnogenic-centaurian-rat in_progress closed --ref c392d736`:\n     20:51:46  COMPLETION_VERDICT commit   <- verifier already finished, no lock held\n     20:51:54  STATUS commit               <- the close itself\n     20:51:56  SIGNATURE commit\n     20:51:59  .ticket-write.lock.d created, stamp pid=85578\n     20:54:37  COMPACT commit              <- lock released\n   i.e. ~2m38s of continuous write-lock hold by `_compact_on_close`, entirely after the LLM.\n   Its stand-aside guard (`write_lock_is_busy`, bug 7084 / R3) is ineffective for the closing\n   process itself: the close released the lock microseconds earlier, so the probe almost always\n   reports \"free\" and compaction proceeds to take it. Bug 7084 measured this holder at 48.1s;\n   on today's store it is minutes, and it scales with store size.\n\nConclusion: the 15+ minute hold observed for pid 61327 was compact-on-close (and/or repeated\nacquisitions), not the verifier. The lock was never held across an LLM call. Implementing the\noperator-specified restructure would be a no-op; fixing the real holder is different work with\ndifferent semantics (whether/when compaction may take the store lock), so it is being raised as\na separate ticket rather than folded in here. Escalating for a decision on how to re-scope this\nbug."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786593319737930001,"uuid":"64afce72-3ca9-48f1-b394-93b54028272d"}